   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Task 3: Model estimation\n",
    "- Fit a logistic regression model with L1 (lasso) regularization on the estimation set\n",
    "- Get predicted probabilities\n",
    "- Compute ROC curve + AUC on the estimation set"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 43,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "lambda_lasso = 3\n",
    "model = LogisticRegression(l1_ratio=1, C=1/lambda_lasso, solver='liblinear', max_iter=500)\n",
    "model = model.fit(features_train, target_train)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7926f7e1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "1. Predicted probabilities"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 44,
   "id": "24de20b9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "p_train = model.predict_proba(features_train)[:, 1]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e19682ed",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "2. ROC curve + AUC, using the classification/AUC chapter"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 45,
   "id": "c2091544",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fpr, tpr, thresholds = roc_curve(target_train, p_train)\n",
    "auc_val = auc(fpr, tpr)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "09b18b9f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "roc_curve slides the cutoff across every possible threshold and, at each one, computes the false-positive-rate and true-positive-rate — literally generating the curve we talked about (the one that should hug the top-left corner for a good model).\n",
    "auc collapses that whole curve into the single summary number, bigger = better separation between \"good\" and \"bad\" applicants."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 46,
   "id": "12277631",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "0.8229513380375449\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(auc_val)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Task 4: Point prediction\n",
    "- Find the statistically optimal cutoff (Youden's J)\n",
    "- Choose a business cutoff (more conservative)\n",
    "- Build point predictions using the cutoff\n",
    "- Compute confusion matrix"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "278507b0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Step A — the statistically \"optimal\" cutoff (Youden's J)\n",
    "\n",
    "Youden's J statistic finds the cutoff that maximizes tpr − fpr; the point on the ROC curve farthest above the useless diagonal line."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 51,
   "id": "c674794c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "optimal_idx = argmax(tpr - fpr)\n",
    "stat_cutoff = thresholds[optimal_idx]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 52,
   "id": "3026559f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "np.float64(0.6210445835789432)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 52,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "stat_cutoff"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e6164033",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Step B — the business cutoff"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ff2727e6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "a false positive (predicting \"good\" when the applicant is actually \"bad\") means the bank loses money on a defaulted loan, much worse than a false negative (rejecting someone who would've paid it back fine). So a business would deliberately want a more conservative (higher) cutoff than the \"equally weighted\" statistical optimum, demanding a very high predicted probability of \"good\" before approving."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 48,
   "id": "14ed3806",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "business_cutoff = 0.72"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "25f3d8fe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Step C — build point predictions and a confusion matrix"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 50,
   "id": "dd6922b0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([[0.25733333, 0.052     ],\n",
       "       [0.22666667, 0.464     ]])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 50,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "x_bar = (p_train >= business_cutoff).astype(int)\n",
    "cm = confusion_matrix(target_train, x_bar, normalize='all')\n",
    "cm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "18d40ce7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "False positive rate ≈ 5.2% — the bank approves a loan to a truly bad-risk applicant only 5.2% of the time, exactly what a conservative cutoff should achieve.\n",
    "False negative rate ≈ 22.7% — but nearly a quarter of genuinely good applicants get rejected. That's the price paid for the conservative stance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Task 5: Model evaluation (out-of-sample)\n",
    "- Apply the fitted model to the test set\n",
