  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "68903838",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Take one random \"good\" applicant and one random \"bad\" applicant from your data. AUC is the probability that the model correctly ranks the good applicant higher than the bad one (i.e., assigns them a higher predicted probability of being good)."
    "\n",
    "AUC = 0.5 → the model is no better than a coin flip. Ranking a random good/bad pair correctly happens exactly half the time, pure chance, no real signal.\n",
    "AUC = 1.0 → perfect. Every single good applicant is ranked above every single bad applicant, no exceptions. Total separation.\n",
    "AUC = 0.78 (the test result) → if I grabbed one random \"good\" and one random \"bad\" applicant, the model ranks them correctly about 78% of the time."
    ]
  },
